IA32 target
-----------
Target values are unsigned u64. The current value is EDX:EAX and the saved left
operand is EBX:ECX. Arithmetic wraps to 64 bits and comparison is unsigned.
All generated control-flow immediates and addresses below are little-endian.

raw32 output = entry_arguments + prologue + statements + halt_code.
efi32 output = pe_header + entry_arguments + prologue + statements + halt_code,
then PE finalization. raw32 has no header. Both output limits are 131072 bytes.

A literal emits immediate (B8), low 32 bits, immediate_high (BA), high 32 bits.
A variable load emits load_local, disp32, load_local_value.
A variable store emits store_local, disp32, store_local_value.
For variable index i starting at 0, disp32 = -8 * (i + 1).
load_local/store_local prefix is LEA EDI,[EBP+disp32]; suffixes transfer two words.
push_rax pushes high then low; pop_rcx pops low then high.

A string emits jump_code + u32 string_length + bytes_including_NUL +
string_address + u32(-(string_length + 7)).
string_address zeroes EDX and computes EAX relative to its call/pop return EIP.
The string_address prefix is 9 bytes; its internal return EIP is 7 bytes after
the literal end. Do not use the template's full length in the displacement.

test_jump emits a combined zero test of EDX:EAX and a JZ prefix, then a rel32.
jump_code emits E9 then a rel32.
For either displacement immediate beginning at offset p and destination d,
patch u32(d - p - 4). This applies to loops and all forward branches.

Entry convention is cdecl with a return address, arg0, arg1 on incoming stack.
The prologue saves arg0/arg1 in the new frame at EBP+0 / EBP+4, reserves 512 bytes
for zeroed locals and 65536 bytes for arena, and stores arena cursor at EBP+8.
arg0/arg1 produce zero-extended pointers. Every load/store checks its pointer
high word is zero; a nonzero high word triggers UD2.
reserve rounds up to 16 bytes and rejects a high size word, integer wrap, or
crossing EBP-512. It preserves the high result word as zero.

call accepts function pointer plus five arguments. call7 accepts pointer plus
seven arguments. Each expression is pushed with push_rax. The call templates
reject a nonzero function-pointer high word, copy each argument's low word to
cdecl stack slots, align ESP, call the function, restore the expression stack,
and return EAX with EDX zero. The ABI functions must preserve EBX/ESI/EDI/EBP.
call template stack cleanup is 48 bytes; call7 cleanup is 64 bytes.
Argument high words are intentionally truncated for this 32-bit firmware ABI.

enter accepts entry, argument, stack; each is pushed as u64. The template checks
all high words are zero, disables interrupts, switches/aligns ESP, provides
cdecl arg0 and arg1=0, then jumps to entry with a generated halt return address.
host_read, host_write, put, exit, default_exit are UD2 placeholders in this
backend. They must be rejected before emission. The historical ELF header
template is also unused by IA32 and does not imply hosted IA32 support.

PE32 header and finalization
---------------------------
Header size: 512. MZ magic: offset0. PE offset field:60, value64.
COFF machine:0x014c. Optional header magic:0x010b, size224.
ImageBase:0x100000. SectionAlignment:4096. FileAlignment:512.
EntryPoint RVA:4096. Subsystem:10 (EFI application).
Two sections: .text (execute/read), .reloc (read/discardable).

All mutable fields below are u32 offsets relative to file/header start:
 92  pe_code_size        rounded raw size of .text
112  pe_data_rva         relocation section RVA
144  pe_image_size       relocation section RVA + 4096
224  pe_reloc_directory  relocation directory RVA
320  pe_virtual_size     exact code byte count after 512-byte header
328  pe_raw_size         rounded raw size of .text
364  pe_reloc_rva        relocation section RVA
372  pe_reloc_offset     relocation section file offset

Let C = current output size - 512.
Let R = align_up(C,512).
Let F = R + 512.
Let V = align_up(C,4096) + 4096.
Patch exact code size C at320; R at92,328; F at372;
V at112,224,364; V+4096 at144.
Zero every padding byte between code end and F+512.
At F store u32(4096); at F+4 store u32(12).
The following two u16 relocation entries are ABSOLUTE zero/no-ops.
Final size is F+512 and must fit 131072 bytes.
Headers already provide directory size12, .reloc virtual size12/raw size512,
.text RVA4096 and .text raw file offset512.

Real-mode target
----------------
Only a real-mode boot image is supported. Output limit: 16384 bytes.
Target values and literals are u32. Reject literals larger than 0xffffffff.
Value register is EAX; saved left operand is ECX. Arithmetic wraps to 32 bits.
There is no 8-bit CPU backend: these code16 encodings require an x86 processor
supporting 32-bit registers and operand/address-size prefixes.

Output = real_header (512 bytes) + prologue + statements + halt_code.
The image is loaded at physical 0x7c00. Header bytes0..4 are EA 00 7E 00 00,
which normalize CS to zero and branch to 0x7e00. Signature at510 is55 AA.
This header does not load later sectors; disk loading policy is in bios.zero.

A literal emits immediate (66 B8) + u32 value.
A load emits load_local (67 66 8B 85) + disp32.
A store emits store_local (67 66 89 85) + disp32.
Variable index i uses disp32 = -4 * (i + 1).
push/pop templates operate on EAX/ECX with 32-bit operand prefixes.

A string emits jump_code + u32 string_length + bytes_including_NUL +
immediate + u32 absolute_literal_start.
If p is the output offset just after emitting immediate, the address is
0x7c00 + p - 2 - string_length. This is absolute, unlike the other backends.
test_jump and jump_code take rel32 with the same d-p-4 patch formula as IA32.
test_jump includes 66 0F84; jump_code is66 E9.

The prologue disables interrupts, clears DS/ES/SS, sets ESP=0x7c00, EBP=0x7000,
preserves incoming DL as a zero-extended drive number at0x6e00, initializes arena
cursor0x6e04 to0x4000, and clears64 locals at0x6f00..0x6fff.
:arg0[] returns the saved BIOS drive number.
reserve uses the physical arena0x4000..0x6000 with16-byte rounding and checks.
The ABI scratch area is0x6e00..0x6eff; temporary stack grows down from0x7c00.

Unsupported real-mode operations include load64/store64, arg1, call/call7,
enter, hosted read/write/put/exit, and a hosted ELF or EFI output mode.
Supported target-specific operations: bios, biosreset, bioscheck, biosmode,
biosput, cpuid, lgdt, cr0/cr3/cr4, rdmsr/wrmsr, longjump, protectedjump, enable.

BIOS disk templates preserve incoming flags, DS/ES and all32-bit general
registers across INT13, enable interrupts for BIOS, clear direction afterward,
and preserve return status through CS:0x6e08. bios uses drive(left) and DAP(right)
for AH42. biosreset uses drive and AX0. bios/biosreset return0 on success,
otherwise AH or256 for CF set with AH zero. bioscheck validates extensions
through AH41, BX55aa -> aa55 and CXbit0; result0 success or1 failure.
Video templates use INT10, AH0 for mode, AH0e/BX7 for character.
cpuid takes leaf and selected output index0..3 (EAX/EBX/ECX/EDX); subleaf is0;
other indexes return0. rdmsr returns only the low32-bit EAX value.
wrmsr evaluates and pushes index, low, high, then pops EDX,EAX,ECX.
lgdt explicitly uses a32-bit base operand.

Far transitions
---------------
longjump and protectedjump each evaluate and push four expressions in this
order: selector, entry, argument, stack.
Emit longjump_setup (32 bytes). Let p be output offset immediately afterward.
Emit u32(0x7c00 + p + 4 + 5), then longjump_branch (5 bytes),
then longjump_abi (35 bytes) or protectedjump_abi (52 bytes).
The patched immediate points exactly at the first byte of the ABI shim.
longjump_setup writes entry at0x6e20, argument at0x6e24, stack at0x6e28,
selector at0x6e30, and ends with the prefix for a dword store to0x6e2c.
The far branch is66 FF 2E 2C 6E (m16:32 far indirect pointer at0x6e2c).
No further relocation patching inside either ABI shim is required.

The64-bit shim clears DS/ES/SS, loads entry into EAX, argument into ECX,
stack into ESP, aligns RSP to16, and jumps to RAX.
The32-bit shim loads selector0x10 into DS/ES/SS/FS/GS, clears direction,
sets stack, aligns it, passes cdecl arg0 plus zero arg1, and dispatches entry.
A returned32-bit kernel reaches its local CLI/HLT loop.

Validation and provenance
-------------------------
Both Zero table assets compiled with the existing own build/zero --host.
A decoder written in Zero emitted every table in ID order. Comparison with
the corresponding instruction symbol ranges in our existing binaries was exact:
  ia32: 1139 decoded bytes, 52 present entries (includes unused ELF header).
  real: 1150 decoded bytes, 52 present entries.
